<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_drop_elabel.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-dropelabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-dropelabel">
  <primary>DROP ELABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP ELABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP ELABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove an edge label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP ELABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropelabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP ELABEL</command> removes an edge label. The table behind it goes too, along
   with its id sequence, its three indexes and its constraints. The label is looked up in the
   graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot carry the graph. Several
   names may be given in one statement.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, which is the default, has two things to object to. A label that
   still holds edges is refused with
   <literal>cannot drop <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> because it is
   not empty.</literal> That is stricter than the dependency check PostgreSQL applies to a
   table, which does not care how many rows there are. A label that has a child label is
   refused as well, and the message names the child.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> overrides both. It drops the label whatever it holds, and deletes
   the label's own edges. The vertices those edges connected are untouched, and so is every
   other edge label. Dropping a <emphasis>vertex</emphasis> label with
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> is the case that reaches further, because it deletes the edges
   attached to that label's vertices whatever labels they have. See
   <xref linkend="cypher-dropvlabel"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The base label <literal>ag_edge</literal> cannot be dropped, and
   <command>DROP ELABEL</command> refuses a vertex label; the statement for one is
   <xref linkend="cypher-dropvlabel"/>. The table behind a label cannot be dropped on its own
   either: <command>DROP TABLE</command> on it is refused and points here. To remove a label's
   edges without dropping the label, write <literal>MATCH ()-[e:<replaceable
   class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>]-&gt;() DELETE e</literal> instead.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropelabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error for a label that does not exist. Other names in the
      same statement are still dropped.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the edge label to drop, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
      Several names may be given, separated by commas.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CASCADE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop the label along with its edges and its child labels. The vertices those edges
      connected are left alone.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RESTRICT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refuse if the label holds any edges or has a child label. This is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropelabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>, but build a
   graph of their own and drop it at the end. An empty label goes without comment, because
   nothing else was affected:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH dropedemo;
SET graph_path = dropedemo;
CREATE VLABEL person;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE ELABEL directed;
CREATE ELABEL unused;
CREATE (:person {name: 'Denis Villeneuve'})-[:directed]-&gt;(:film {title: 'Arrival'});
DROP ELABEL unused;

DROP ELABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>directed</literal> has an edge in it, so the default refuses:
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL directed;

ERROR:  cannot drop directed because it is not empty.
</programlisting>
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> takes the label and its edge. Both vertices are still there:
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL directed CASCADE;
MATCH (n) RETURN count(n) AS vertices;

 vertices
----------
 2
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A label with a child is refused too, and <literal>CASCADE</literal> names the child it takes
   with it:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL worked_on;
CREATE ELABEL acted_in INHERITS (worked_on);
DROP ELABEL worked_on;

ERROR:  cannot drop elabel worked_on because other objects depend on it
DETAIL:  elabel acted_in depends on elabel worked_on
HINT:  Use DROP ... CASCADE to drop the dependent objects too.
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL worked_on CASCADE;

NOTICE:  drop cascades to elabel acted_in
DROP ELABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Several labels can be named at once, and <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> passes over the ones
   that are already gone:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL a;
CREATE ELABEL b;
DROP ELABEL IF EXISTS a, b, c;

NOTICE:  elabel "c" does not exist, skipping
DROP ELABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Refusals worth recognizing. Each statement drops one kind of label, the base labels stay, and
   the table cannot be dropped out from under the label:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL kept;
DROP VLABEL kept;

ERROR:  DROP VLABEL cannot DROP edge label
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL person;

ERROR:  DROP ELABEL cannot DROP vertex label
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL ag_edge;

ERROR:  DROP base edge label is prohibited
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP TABLE dropedemo.kept;

ERROR:  cannot drop table dropedemo.kept because elabel kept requires it
HINT:  You can drop elabel kept instead.
</programlisting>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH dropedemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropelabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   Dropping an edge label leaves nothing dangling. An edge points at its endpoints and never the
   other way round, so no vertex holds a reference to a removed edge.
   <structfield>start</structfield> and <structfield>end</structfield> are plain
   <type>graphid</type> columns rather than foreign keys, and it is the graph write path, not a
   referential action on the table, that keeps the two sides in step.
  </para>

  <para>
   <varname>cypher_allow_unsafe_ddl</varname> switches off the kind check and the base label
   check, so with it on <command>DROP ELABEL</command> will drop a vertex label and
   <literal>ag_edge</literal> can be dropped. It exists to repair a graph whose catalog is
   already wrong. Leave it off otherwise. See <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropelabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no statement that drops a label. Element types belong to a graph type,
   which is dropped whole by the drop graph type statement of clause 12.7, and dropping one
   deletes no data.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP ELABEL</command> corresponds to <command>DROP TABLE</command>, and
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>, the name list and <literal>CASCADE</literal> or
   <literal>RESTRICT</literal> behave as they do there. The one addition has no
   <command>DROP TABLE</command> counterpart: a label that is not empty is refused unless
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> is written, where <command>DROP TABLE</command> does not care how
   many rows a table has.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

